# 1 --------------------------------------------------- tension cue structure (yMJaUS776yQ)
S("yMJaUS776yQ","How to Write Investigative Tension Cues | 52 Cues Podcast, 2025 Week 34",
 "light-tension-cue-aba-every-4-bars-energy-pulse-engine-mediant-chord-lever-breakdown-simmer-a-plus-b-return-cliffhanger-edit-point",
 "Light/investigative tension cue form: one-chord drone + offset pulse 'rhythmic engine', a new gear every 4 bars (new layer or octaves), a mediant chord change as the last lever before an ABA breakdown that simmers, and a final A that is really A+B, ending on a delay-free button",
 "Write a 'light tension' cue (analysis, deduction, assembling clues) where no chord progression drives the music: hold one minor chord as a drone, replace the harmonic engine with interlocking synth pulses placed off the strong beats, add a gear of energy every 4 bars (a new layer, or octaves/harmonies on an existing part), use a mediant root move (i -> VI, later iv) as the last lever before the breakdown, make B a breakdown (development by reduction) that simmers and rebuilds, and return with an A that combines A and B material with no new sounds, more octaves, denser drums and booms every 2 bars into a final button.",
 ["A DAW with MIDI; mallet/pluck lead, dark piano or keys, 2-3 synth pulse patches, a noise-based tick patch, a sustained pad/drone, a small sample drum kit","A stereo delay with independent L/R times and a filter on the repeats","One-shot boom/impact and a cymbal-swell or noise riser"],
 [("key","string","Minor key for the drone, e.g. F minor",True),("hook","midi","Short syncopated melodic hook with space for a response",True),("tempo","number","Tempo in BPM",True)],
 [("cue","audio","ABA tension cue ending on a clean button"),("form_map","notes","Bar map: 4-bar gears, chord-lever bar, breakdown, A+B return, edit points")],
 ["Harmony rule from the transcript: modal/minor, hanging on one chord for several phrases; chord changes are saved as tension/release events.",
  "Mediant moves share common tones (F minor F-Ab-C -> Db major Db-F-Ab), so the same melody fits over i, VI and iv without rewriting.",
  "The presenter bends the drone pad's pitch and runs it through tremolo; for Matt keep pitch steady (no pitch bends on the drone); use filter or level movement instead.",
  "Instrument/library choices in the source (specific commercial patches and sample packs) are not required; any mallet, keys, pulse synth and noise tick will do.",
  "No new sounds in the final A section; density comes from octaves, combining A and B material and busier drums.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Pick a minor key and hold one chord (i) as a drone for several phrases. Write a short, syncopated melodic hook first (mallet-type lead), leaving space after each phrase.","Keys/mallet + pad","Hook over a static minor chord"),
  ("Put a stereo delay on the lead: left a dotted 1/8, right a 1/4, with a low-pass filter on the repeats so each echo darkens. This adds 1/16-note inner motion for free.","Stereo delay with filtered feedback","Lead bounces L/R with darkening echoes"),
  ("Fill the space with call and response: the lead calls, a dark piano/keys answers with a counter-line (a little chromaticism), and a third voice (metal/tank-drum-like pluck, also on the delay) completes the final response.","Second and third melodic voices","Three-part call/response phrase"),
  ("Build the rhythmic engine instead of chord changes: a downbeat bass pulse, with a bass mirroring the low piano roots in the same dotted rhythm, then noise 'ticks' accenting the end of each beat, and a second synth pulse also pushed onto the off-beat 'and'. Offset every pulse so nothing stacks on the strong downbeats.","Pulse synths + noise tick patch","Interlocking pulses; groove feels driven without chord motion"),
  ("Every 4 bars add one gear: a new layer (second pulse, ticks, light backbeat, bass) or octaves/harmonies on an existing part. Put a boom on the downbeat of each new double phrase (every 8 bars), on every other phrase.","Arrangement","Energy rises every 4 bars without getting cluttered"),
  ("When there are no new layers left, pull the chord lever: move i to VI (mediant, e.g. Fm -> Db) right before the breakdown, keeping the same melody.","Chord change","Same melody sounds brighter and lifted over VI"),
  ("Breakdown (B = breakdown, not bridge): enter with a cymbal swell/noise riser and a boom on the downbeat to mark arrival, then strip density, let it simmer, sprinkle a little of the main melody on the piano, add an octave to the pad, then bring pulses back and build.","Riser + boom + mutes","Development by reduction, then rebuild"),
  ("End the breakdown on a cliffhanger edit point (unresolved, e.g. a flat-2 note borrowed from Phrygian) and always sweep into it with a whoosh/reverse; automate delays off so no echoes ring into the edit point.","Reverse/whoosh + delay automation","Clean, editable cliffhanger"),
  ("Return to A as A+B: restart from the energy where you left (VI), alternate VI -> i, then resolve to iv instead of i (minor plagal twist). Combine the B-section melody with A material, add octaves (single -> double -> triple, high keys doubling the lead; when stacking upward lower velocities), densify drums, booms every 2 bars.","Arrangement","Final section peaks with no new sounds"),
  ("Finish on a button with a final boom; automate delay sends off so nothing trails past the button.","Delay automation","Clean final hit")],
 ["Form map shows a new layer or octave addition at every 4-bar boundary through A.","Chord changes occur only at the planned lever points (i -> VI before B; VI/i/iv in the return).","No delay tail continues past the edit point or the final button (check the waveform after the last hit).","Final A introduces no instrument that was not heard earlier."],
 ["arrangement","tension","cue-structure","aba-form","breakdown","pulse","mediant","edit-point","call-response","octave-stacking"])

# 2 --------------------------------------------------- melodic cells + octatonic (efnKT5pHqUo)
S("efnKT5pHqUo","Decode ACTION CUES with Melodic Cells",
 "melodic-cells-ostinato-repetition-legitimizes-tonic-against-bass-tension-major-triad-release-octatonic-half-step-cycle",
 "Action/tension cue from melodic cells: build short rhythmic-melodic cells, reorder and transpose them (up minor thirds for tension), repeat them so they sound intentional, put tension in the bass against a tonic ostinato, release with major triads, and derive octatonic (diminished) chords by cycling the cell down half steps",
 "Write a short tense cue without a long melody: derive short melodic cells (a few pitches with a fixed interval shape) from a main ostinato, re-voice them in new orders and rhythms, transpose them up by minor thirds to raise tension, repeat cells so fast ideas sound deliberate, fix a simple non-random form, add tension by moving the bass against the ostinato's tonic and release it with major triads over the minor music, and add darker colour by blending the minor scale with the octatonic scale obtained by playing the cell four times each a half step lower.",
 ["A DAW or notation tool with MIDI","Any string/brass/woodwind or synth patches for ostinato, counter-cells and chords"],
 [("ostinato","midi","Main ostinato in a minor key (4 pitches is enough)",True),("form","string","Simple section order, e.g. A A B A",True)],
 [("cell_set","midi","Main cell plus reordered and transposed variants"),("cue","audio","Short cue with tension and release moments")],
 ["Core rule from the transcript: repetition legitimizes; repeat cells so they are heard as intentional.",
  "Keep the form simple and predictable even when the material is busy; nothing completely random.",
  "Do not repeat a section exactly twice in a row; recontextualise (new bass/harmony under the same cell).",
  "Place the more dissonant passages in predictable spots (e.g. always right after the hook).",
  "Steady pitch and fixed notes; the cells are composed, not randomised (Matt's preference).",ABL],
 [("Write the main ostinato and pull out its pitch set (e.g. 4 notes). Write new cells from the same pitches in a different order and rhythm for another voice (e.g. horn).","MIDI editor","2-3 cells sharing one pitch set"),
  ("Reduce a cell to its interval shape and transpose it; for tension move it up by minor thirds in a higher voice (strings).","Transpose","Same cell, rising in minor thirds"),
  ("Repeat the cells often enough that each fast idea is heard as deliberate; choose a simple form and stick to it.","Arrangement","Recognisable identity across the cue"),
  ("Recontextualise on repeats: change the bass under the ostinato so it fights the ostinato's strong tonic (tension); to release, put major triads over the heavily minor ostinato.","Bass line + chord layer","Audible tension and release on the same ostinato"),
  ("Derive the octatonic scale: play the cell four times in a row, each starting a half step lower; the collected pitches form a diminished (octatonic) scale. Blend it with the minor scale (shared notes) and borrow its clashes for tension chords; space a semitone clash into a major 7th so it sounds intentional.","Scale/chord derivation","Darker chord colours that still sit with the minor ostinato"),
  ("Optionally alternate between octatonic collections for major/minor triads over the ostinato to get ambiguous, uneasy colour.","Chord layer","Ambiguous but controlled harmony")],
 ["Every melodic idea in the cue is traceable to the main cell (same pitch set or interval shape).","At least one passage transposes a cell up by minor thirds.","A major triad appears at the planned release point over the minor ostinato.","The tension-chord pitches are all inside one octatonic collection derived from the half-step cycle."],
 ["melody","melodic-cells","ostinato","octatonic","diminished-scale","tension","release","action-cue","minor-thirds"])

# 3 --------------------------------------------------- tension/release harmonic plan (PUycGHv-2Jo)
S("PUycGHv-2Jo","Tension and Release: How to Add Emotional Impact To Your Music",
 "sus4-tension-rising-key-steps-pedal-bvi-bvii-over-pedal-big-tonic-release-widening-range-unison-to-octaves",
 "Tension-to-release build: sus4 dissonance released on the last note, a theme stated on the dominant then the tonic, rising key steps, an Ab pedal with Bb over it (bVI then bVII over a bVI pedal) resolving to a big C, while dynamics and pitch range widen and the theme moves from unison to octaves",
 "Turn a plain theme into an emotional build that holds back release: replace the plain chord with a sus4 and release it as the melody lands, introduce the theme first on the dominant (G sus4) and then the tonic, step the key upward to raise anticipation, then hold a low pedal on bVI (Ab in C) with a bVII (Bb) chord on top for two bars each, and finally resolve to a big tonic (C) with a timpani-style roll into it, while dynamics grow, the pitch range opens out from narrow to wide, and the theme goes from unison doubling to octaves.",
 ["A DAW with orchestral or synth string/brass/woodwind patches (any library or synths)","A roll/swell (timpani, noise swell) and a rising gliss or riser bounced to audio"],
 [("key","string","Home key, e.g. C major",True),("theme","midi","A simple short theme",True)],
 [("build","audio","Section that builds anticipation and releases onto the tonic"),("harmony_plan","notes","Chord/key steps with bar counts")],
 ["Release only at key points; build anticipation first (the transcript: don't resolve in two and a half seconds).",
  "Chord/key steps (G sus4 -> C -> Eb -> Ab pedal, Bb over Ab -> C) are the presenter's sketch in C; transpose freely.",
  "Specific sample libraries in the source are not required.",
  "Steady pitch; no random note variation (Matt's preference).",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Contrast: play the theme over a plain major chord, then over a sus4 that resolves to the major exactly as the melody lands on its last note.","Chord layer","Small dissonance-release that adds impact"),
  ("Open with anticipation: short repeated string figure (tremolo/trim) and a counter-line, then state the theme first on the dominant (sus4) and then in the tonic.","Strings + woodwind lead","Theme teased before its full statement"),
  ("Raise tension by stepping the key/harmony up at phrase ends (in the sketch: C -> Eb, then up again) while adding horns and light percussion. No release yet.","Harmony + orchestration","Rising anticipation"),
  ("Final approach: a low pedal on bVI (Ab in C) held for 2 bars, then a bVII (Bb) chord over the same pedal for 2 bars with added movement, then resolve onto a big tonic chord, with a roll (timpani or noise swell) leading into it.","Low strings/bass pedal + chords + roll","Strong arrival on the tonic"),
  ("Grow dynamics and widen pitch range throughout: start narrow and soft, end wide (low brass/tuba under, high parts above). Double the theme in unison the first time, then in octaves once the bVII arrives.","Orchestration","Build feels bigger, not just louder"),
  ("Add rising gliss/riser effects as audio clips placed exactly where needed (bounce them out first for precise placement).","Audio clip","Ear candy lands exactly on the transitions")],
 ["The tonic chord is withheld until the planned release point.","Harmonic plan contains a bVI pedal with bVII over it in the 4 bars before release.","Pitch range (lowest to highest note) is wider at the release than at the start."],
 ["tension","release","harmony","sus4","pedal-point","key-change","build","orchestration","bVI-bVII-I"])

# 4 --------------------------------------------------- phaser practical uses (xO9CiDa3324)
S("xO9CiDa3324","How To Use Phasers In Your Songs",
 "phaser-static-notch-tone-shaping-few-notch-slow-swirl-pad-hat-loop-phaser-after-delay-send-section-bus-phaser-variation",
 "Phaser as a mix and arrangement tool (delta on our phaser pads): static notch-centre tone shaping with LFO depth at zero, few-notch high-spread slow swirl on pads, a phaser after the delay send to clear the centre, and a bus phaser switched on only for a pre-chorus/outro or phrase-end ear candy",
 "Use a phaser on sustained, harmonically rich sources (not short or narrow-band ones). Delta beyond our existing phaser-pad variants: (1) tone-shape a bass by setting the LFO amount to zero and sweeping only the notch centre (low notch count; more notches/feedback ~50% for extreme), (2) keep pads to ~3 notches with high spread and a slow LFO so they swirl L/R and leave the centre free, (3) put a slow 4-notch phaser after a delay (or reverb) on a send so the echoes spread away from the centre element, (4) automate a phaser on the bus for one section (pre-chorus, outro) or a phrase end as variation.",
 ["A DAW with a phaser that exposes notches/stages, centre frequency, spread, feedback, LFO rate/amount and dry/wet","Send/return tracks"],
 [("source","audio","Sustained harmonically rich source (pad, bass, loose hat loop, delay return)",True)],
 [("phased_parts","audio","Bass tone variants, swirling pad, phased delay return, section variation"),("automation","notes","Section on/off and dry/wet automation")],
 ["Works best on sounds that are steady in level and full-spectrum; little effect on short, narrow-band sounds.",
  "Fewer notches = smoother; more notches = more 'ridgy'/wavy. Keep pads to low notch counts.",
  "Keep LFO slow on held pads so the pitch/tone movement stays gentle (Matt dislikes wobbly sounds); tone-shaping mode uses no LFO at all.",
  "Hat-loop phasing (5 notches, feedback up) suits loose, open hats; avoid making a rapid clicky texture (Matt's preference).",ABL],
 [("Bass tone shaping: phaser on a synth bass, ~3 notches, LFO amount 0; sweep only the centre frequency to audition tones and keep the one you like. For extreme colour lower spread, raise feedback to ~50%, 4-5 notches; blend with dry/wet (automatable).","Phaser (static)","Distinct bass tones without movement"),
  ("Pad life: ~3 notches, spread high, amount up, slow LFO so the left/right phase relationship swirls; check on headphones; raise rate only for a stronger effect.","Phaser on pad","Pad swirls in stereo and leaves the centre open"),
  ("Hat loop: phaser with ~5 notches, feedback up to hear notch resonances, slow LFO, centre frequency in the hats' upper range; use on looser, fuller hats.","Phaser on hat loop","Stale loop gets slow movement"),
  ("Send trick: on a delay (or reverb) return, add a slow 4-notch phaser after the delay so repeats smear around the stereo image and clear room for the centre element.","Return track: delay -> phaser","Centre element clearer; echoes wider"),
  ("Arrangement variation: automate a phaser on the bus (everything except the lead) for a single section such as the pre-chorus or outro, or for the last beat of a phrase as ear candy.","Bus phaser + automation","Section contrast without new parts")],
 ["Bass tone-shaping render has no periodic movement (LFO amount 0).","Pad with phaser keeps correlation >= 0 while swirling.","Bus phaser is active only in the automated section(s)."],
 ["phaser","pad","stereo","send-fx","delay","bass-tone","arrangement-variation","ear-candy"])
